{"name":"White Geranium","hex":"#f1f1e1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"geranium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any flowering plant of the genus Geranium, of family Geraniaceae.","Any flowering plant of the genus Pelargonium, of family Geraniaceae."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bright red color tinted with orange, like that of a scarlet geranium (Pelargonium inquinans)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/geranium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Geranium","blocks":[{"text":"Geranium is a genus of 422 species of annual, biennial, and perennial plants that are commonly known as geraniums, hardy geraniums, or cranesbills. They are found throughout the temperate regions of the world and the mountains of the tropics, with the greatest diversity in the eastern part of the Mediterranean region."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Geranium_February_2008-1.jpg/330px-Geranium_February_2008-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geranium","wikidata":"Q157211"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Geranium_sanguineum02.jpg/500px-Geranium_sanguineum02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Geranium_sanguineum02.jpg/500px-Geranium_sanguineum02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The \"bill\" and seed dispersal mechanism of G. pratense","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeranium_sanguineum02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Geranium_%28probably_%27Johnson%27s_Blue%27%29.jpg/500px-Geranium_%28probably_%27Johnson%27s_Blue%27%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Geranium_%28probably_%27Johnson%27s_Blue%27%29.jpg/1280px-Geranium_%28probably_%27Johnson%27s_Blue%27%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Geranium_%28probably_%27Johnson%27s_Blue%27%29.jpg/500px-Geranium_%28probably_%27Johnson%27s_Blue%27%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close up of a wet geranium cultivar (probably 'Johnson's Blue') ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeranium_(probably_'Johnson's_Blue').jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Red_Geranium_in_Nepal.jpg/120px-Red_Geranium_in_Nepal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.o